      'Prints Message mesno.' +
      LineEnding +
      'Prints message between quotes.' +
      LineEnding +
      'Please notice you can either user double or single quotes for the string but use the same for starting and ending.' +
      LineEnding +
      'MES "Hello World!" MES ''I''ll be there at 3 o''clock.'' MES "John says "Hello"."',
    'MESSAGE mesno.' +
      LineEnding +
      'MESSAGE mesno. or MESSAGE "string"' +
      LineEnding +
      'Prints Message mesno. After printing either message, it prints a new line.' +
      LineEnding +
      'Prints message between quotes. After printing either message, it prints a new line.' +
      LineEnding +
      'Please notice you can either user double or single quotes for the string but use the same for starting and ending.',
    'MINUS flagno. value' +
      LineEnding +
      'Flag flagno. is decreased by value. If the result is negative the flag is set to 0.',
    'MODE option' +
      LineEnding +
      'Allows the current window to have its operation flags changed. To calculate the number to use for the option just add the numbers shown next to each item to achieve the required combination.' +
      LineEnding +
      '1 - Use the alternative character set. (A permanent #g, see escape sequences below)' +
      LineEnding +
      '2 - SM32 ("More...") will not appear when the window fills.' +
      LineEnding +
      'e.g. MODE 3 stops the ''More...'' prompt and causes all characters to be translated to the 128-256 range.',
    'MOUSE value value' +
      LineEnding +
      'Only available in PC/DOS and HTML. Does nothing in other targets.' +
      LineEnding +
      'The second value indicates an action to perform with the mouse. The first is a parameter for that action.' +
      LineEnding +
      'ActionParametet 1Parameter 2 Reset mouse (DOS only)Any0 Show pointerAny1 Show pointerAny2 Read position: the flag in the first parameter is the first in a list of flags. That flag will contain the sta' +
      'te of the buttons, the next flag will contain the X position divided by 8 (in characters), and the next the Y position divided by 8 (the row). In the next flag the X position divided by 6 (narrow colu' +
      'mns) will be stored.flagno3 Read position(fine) : Similar to the previous one, but in the flag + 1 the X position divided by 2 will be stored if we are in VGA mode, or divided by 4 if it is SVGA, and ' +
      'in the flag + 2 the Y position divided by two in SVGA mode, and not divided if it is VGA.flagno4 Modify pointer (DOS only): load the file with PTR extension defined by first parameter. If first parame' +
      'ter is 1, it loads 001.PTRPointer number5 Changes hotspot coord X value (DOS only): originally the hotspot in the pointer is at x=0, y=0, that is the upper left corner of the opinter. If you change th' +
      'e pointer maybe you would like to move the hotspot. For instance, if the pointer is a cross, you may want to put it at 5,5, which is the center of the 9x9 area.X6 Changes hotspot coord Y value (DOS on' +
      'ly): same as before, but for Y value.Y7' +
      LineEnding +
      'The flag that contains the current status of the buttons will contain a 1 if the left button is pressed, a 2 for the right and a 4 for the center. If there is more than one pressed the values are adde' +
      'd. For example, left and right at the same time, the value will be 3.' +
      LineEnding +
      'PTR files are 81 bytes long, 9x9, each byte represents a pixel in a 9x9 square. Color 0 is transparent.' +
      LineEnding +
      'For instance, this code would allow you to use the mouse in DAAD:' +
      LineEnding +
      'MOUSE 0 0 ; Resets mouse' +
      LineEnding +
      'MOUSE 0 1 ; Shows pointer' +
      LineEnding +
      'MOUSE 100 4; Reads fine position in flags 100, 101 and 102' +
      LineEnding +
      'There are specific symbols in the compiler that allow us to not remember the action numbers. See the symbols in the appendices.',
    'MOVE flagno.' +
      LineEnding +
      'This is a very powerful action designed to manipulate PSI''s. It allows the current player order verb to be used to scan the connections section for the location given in Flag flagno. If the Verb is fo' +
      'und then Flag flagno. is changed to be the location number associated with it, and the next condact is considered. If the verb is not found, or the original location number was invalid, then DAAD cons' +
      'iders the next entry in the table - if present.' +
      LineEnding +
      'This feature could be used to provide characters with random movement in valid directions; by setting the player order verb to a random movement word and allowing MOVE to decide if the character can g' +
      'o that way. Note that any special movements which are dealt with in Response for the player, must be dealt with separately for a PSI as well.',
    'NEWLINE' +
      LineEnding +
      'Prints a carriage return/line feed.' +
      LineEnding +
      'So what''s the difference between DESC/MES/SYSMESS/MESSAGE when used with a string? Basically, the compiler adds internally that new message to the corresponding table, so MES/MESSAGE will add that mes' +
      'sage to MTX table (user messages), SYSMESS will add it to STX table (system messages) and DESC to LTX table (Locations). In the end it doesn''t really matter, but take in mind each table has a limit of' +
      ' 256 messages, so you can use SYSMESS or DESC if you run out of space in MTX table.' +
      LineEnding +
      'The truth is the new DRC compiler already does that for you, so you can always use MES/MESSAGE and forget about it, the compiler will automatically put the message in the table where there is still so' +
      'me space.',
    'NEWTEXT' +
      LineEnding +
      'Forces the loss of any remaining phrases on the current input line. You would use this to prevent the player continuing without a fresh input should something go badly for his situation. e.g. the GET ' +
      'action carries out a NEWTEXT if it fails to get the required object for any reason, to prevent disaster with a sentence such as:' +
      LineEnding +
      'GET SWORD AND KILL ORC WITH IT' +
      LineEnding +
      'as attacking the ORC without the sword may be dangerous!',
    'NOTAT locno' +
      LineEnding +
      'Succeeds if the current location is different to locno.',
    'NOTCARR objno.' +
      LineEnding +
      'Succeeds if Object objno. is not carried.',
    'NOTDONE' +
      LineEnding +
      'This action jumps to the end of the process table and flags DAAD that #no# action has been carried out. i.e. no more condacts or entries are considered. A return will thus be made to the previous call' +
      'ing process table or to the start point of any active DOALL loop. This will cause DAAD to print one of the "I can''t" messages if needed. i.e. if no other action is carried out and no entry is present ' +
      'in the connections section for the current Verb.',
    'NOTEQ flagno. value' +
      LineEnding +
      'Succeeds if flag flagno. is not equal to value.',
    'NOTSAME flagno1. flagno2.' +
      LineEnding +
      'Succeeds if flag flagno1 does not have the same value as flag flagno2.',
    'NOTWORN objno.' +
      LineEnding +
      'Succeeds if Object objno. is not worn.',
    'NOTZERO flagno.' +
      LineEnding +
      'Succeeds if flag flagno. is not set to zero.',
    'NOUN2 word' +
      LineEnding +
      'Succeeds if the second noun in the current player order is word.',
    'OK' +
      LineEnding +
      'SM15 ("OK") is printed and action DONE is performed.',
    'PAPER colour' +
      LineEnding +
      'Sets current window paper colour according to the lookup table given in the graphics editors, or as a palette number for machines with a palette.',
    'PARSE n' +
      LineEnding +
      'The parameter ''n'' controls which level of string indentation is to be searched. Now only two are supported by the interpreters so only the values 0 and 1 are valid.' +
      LineEnding +
      '0 - Parse the main input line for the next LS.' +
      LineEnding +
      '1 - Parse any string (phrase enclosed in quotes [""]) that was contained in the last player order extracted.' +
      LineEnding +
      'Mode 0 is the primary method for converting the current input line of the player to a logical sentence (LS). The detailed description of the parser provides further details. The command will extract t' +
      'he next player order from the current input line.' +
      LineEnding +
      'Mode 1 was designed to deal with speech to PSIs. Any string (i.e. a further phrase enclosed in quotes [""]) that was present in the players current phrase is converted into a player order- overwriting' +
      ' the existing player order formed originally for that phrase.' +
      LineEnding +
      'If no phrase is present at level 0 then the input line is called preceded with a random prompt - this gets a new input line from the player automatically when required, removing the problem of handlin' +
      'g multiple phrases by the programmer. At level 1 and above the next CondAct is carried out. This occurs at all levels if the player order is invalid. Note that DAAD will look at the next condact in wh' +
      'at can be considered a fail situation - this is different to what you might expect. Otherwise the next entry is considered with the new player order of the speech made to the PSI. Because it overwrite' +
      's the current player order it must be used carefully - this is also, the reason for doing the next CondAct in a fail situation, think about it!' +
      LineEnding +
      'If you are using a text-based command structure you will need at least one PARSE 0 CondAct in the main loop somewhere.' +
      LineEnding +
      'e.g. the minimum process 0 without an initialization might be: -' +
      LineEnding +
      '> _ _' +
      LineEnding +
      'PARSE 0' +
      LineEnding +
      'MESSAGE "I don''t understand"' +
      LineEnding +
      'REDO' +
      LineEnding +
      '> _ _' +
      LineEnding +
      'PROCESS x ; Deal with any commands' +
      LineEnding +
      'REDO' +
      LineEnding +
      'To use it to speak to a PSI there will be two or more calling entries (in process x of the above example) which will be similar to:' +
      LineEnding +
      '> SAY name' +
      LineEnding +
      'SAME pos 38 ;Are they here?' +
      LineEnding +
      'PROCESS y ;Decode speech?' +
      LineEnding +
      'DONE ;player orderdestroyed so always DONE.' +
      LineEnding +
      '> SAY name' +
      LineEnding +
      'MESSAGE z ;"They are not here!"' +
      LineEnding +
      'DONE' +
      LineEnding +
      'With a "PROCESS y" similar to:' +
      LineEnding +
      '> _ _' +
      LineEnding +
      'PARSE ;Always do this entry' +
      LineEnding +
      'MESSAGE x ;"They don''t understand"' +
      LineEnding +
      'DONE' +
      LineEnding +
      '> word word' +
      LineEnding +
      'CondAct list ;Any phrases PSI understands' +
      LineEnding +
      '> _ _' +
      LineEnding +
      'MESSAGE x ;as above or different message',
    'PAUSE value' +
      LineEnding +
      'Pauses for value/50 secs. However, if value is zero then game stops until a key is pressed. Then flags 60 & 61 will be set top the code of the key pressed. Please notice most 8 bit machines only updat' +
      'e flag 60.',
    'PICTURE picno' +
      LineEnding +
      'Checks whether the graphic whose number is picno, exists. If so, the next condact is executed, if not, it goes to the next entry. Also, in targets where there is RAM enough to keep a copy of graphics ' +
      '(in general, machines with more than 64K), it loads the graphic in RAM, but doesn''t paint it on the screen.',
    'PLACE objno. locno+' +
      LineEnding +
      'The position of Object objno. is changed to Location locno. Flag 1 is decremented if the object was carried. It is incremented if the object is placed at location 254 (carried).',
    'PLUS flagno. value' +
      LineEnding +
      'Flag flagno. is increased by value. If the result exceeds 255 the flag is set to 255.',
    'PREP word' +
      LineEnding +
      'Succeeds if the preposition in the current player order is word.',
    'PRESENT objno.' +
      LineEnding +
      'Succeeds if Object objno. is carried, worn or at the current location.',
    'PRINT flagno.' +
      LineEnding +
      'The value of Flag flagno. is displayed without leading or trailing spaces. This is a very useful action. Say flag 100 contained the number of coins carried by the player, then an entry in a process ta' +
      'ble of:' +
      LineEnding +
      'MES "You have "' +
      LineEnding +
      'PRINT 100' +
      LineEnding +
      'MESSAGE " gold coins."' +
      LineEnding +
      'could be used to display this to the player.',
    'PRINTAT line col' +
      LineEnding +
      'Sets current print position to given point if in current window. If not, then print position becomes top left of window.',
    'PROCESS procno.' +
      LineEnding +
      'This powerful action transfers the attention of DAAD to the specified Process table number. Note that it is a true subroutine call and any exit from the new table (e.g. DONE, OK etc.) will return cont' +
      'rol to the condact which follows the calling PROCESS action. A sub-process can call (nest) further process'' to a depth of 10 at which point a runtime error will be generated.',
    'PUTIN objno. locno+' +
      LineEnding +
      'If Object objno. is worn then SM24 ("I can''t. I''m wearing the _.") is printed and actions NEWTEXT & DONE are performed.' +
      LineEnding +
      'If Object objno. is at the current location (but neither worn nor carried), SM49 ("I don''t have the _.") is printed and actions NEWTEXT & DONE are performed.' +
      LineEnding +
      'If Object objno. is not at the current location, but not carried, then SM28 ("I don''t have one of those.") is printed and actions NEWTEXT & DONE are performed.' +
      LineEnding +
      'Otherwise the position of Object objno. is changed to Location locno. Flag 1 is decremented and SM44 ("The _ is in the"), a description of Object locno. and SM51 (".") is printed.',
    'PUTO locno+' +
      LineEnding +
      'The position of the currently referenced object (i.e. that object whose number is given in flag 51), is changed to be Location locno. Flag 54 remains its old location. Flag 1 is decremented if the obj' +
      'ect was carried. It is incremented if the object is placed at location 254 (carried).',
    'QUIT' +
      LineEnding +
      'SM12 ("Are you sure?") is printed and the input routine called. Will succeed if the player replies with a word which starts with the first letter of SM30 ("Y") to the prompt. If not then the remainder' +
      ' of the entry is discarded and the next entry is carried out.',
    'RAMLOAD flagno.' +
      LineEnding +
      'This action is the counterpart of RAMSAVE and allows the saved buffer to be restored. The parameter specifies the last flag to be reloaded which can be used to preserve values over a restore, for exam' +
      'ple, an entry of:' +
      LineEnding +
      '> RAMLO _' +
      LineEnding +
      'COPYFF 30 255' +
      LineEnding +
      'RAMLOAD 254' +
      LineEnding +
      'COPYFF 255 30' +
      LineEnding +
      'RESTART' +
      LineEnding +
      'could be used to maintain the current score, so that the player can not use RAMSAVE/LOAD as an easy option for achieving 100%!' +
      LineEnding +
      'Note 1: The RAM actions could be used to implement an OOPS command that is common on other systems to take back the previous move; by creating an entry in the main loop which does an automatic RAMSAVE' +
      ' every time the player enters a move.' +
      LineEnding +
      'Note 2: These four actions allow the next CondAct to be carried out. They should normally always be followed by a RESTART or describe in order that the game state is restored to an identical position.',
    'RAMSAVE' +
      LineEnding +
      'In a similar way to SAVE this action saves all the information relevant to the game in progress not onto disc but into a memory buffer. This buffer is of course volatile and will be destroyed when the' +
      ' machine is turned off which should be made clear to the player. The next action is always carried out.',
    'RANDOM flagno.' +
      LineEnding +
      'Flag flagno. is set to a number from the Pseudo-random sequence from 1 to 100. This could be useful to allow random decisions to be made in a more flexible way than with the CHANCE condition.',
    'REDO' +
      LineEnding +
      'Will restart the currently executing table.',
    'REMOVE objno.' +
      LineEnding +
      'If Object objno. is carried or at the current location (but not worn) then SM50 ("I''m not wearing the _.") is printed and actions NEWTEXT & DONE are performed.' +
      LineEnding +
      'If Object objno. is not at the current location, SM23 ("I''m not wearing one of those.") is printed and actions NEWTEXT & DONE are performed.' +
      LineEnding +
      'If Object objno. is not wearable (and thus removable) then SM41 ("I can''t remove the _.") is printed and actions NEWTEXT & DONE are performed.' +
      LineEnding +
      'If the maximum number of objects is being carried (Flag 1 is greater than, or the same as, Flag 37), SM42 ("I can''t remove the _. My hands are full.") is printed and actions NEWTEXT & DONE are perform' +
      'ed.' +
      LineEnding +
      'Otherwise the position of Object objno. is changed to carried. Flag 1 is incremented and SM38 ("I''ve removed the _.") printed.',
    'RESET' +
      LineEnding +
      'This Action bears no resemblance to the one with the same name in PAW. It has the pure function of placing all objects at the position given in the Object start table. It also sets the relevant flags ' +
      'dealing with no of objects carried etc.',
    'RESTART' +
      LineEnding +
      'Will cancel any DOALL loop, any sub-process calls and make a jump to execute process 0 again from the start.',
    'SAME flagno1. flagno2.' +
      LineEnding +
      'Succeeds if flag flagno1 has the same value as flag flagno2.',
    'SAVE opt' +
      LineEnding +
      'SAVE opt This action saves the current game position on disc or tape. SM60 ("Type in name of file.") is printed and the input routine is called to get the filename from the player. If the supplied fil' +
      'ename is not acceptable SM59 ("File name error.") is printed - this is not checked on 8-bit machines, the file name is MADE acceptable!' +
      LineEnding +
      'Option parameter ask DAAD to do one of the following, but some targets may ignore it and have just tape or just disc support.' +
      LineEnding +
      '0 - Ask user tape or disc' +
      LineEnding +
      '1 - Save to TAPE (i.e. does not ask "Disc or Tape?")' +
      LineEnding +
      '2 - Save to DISC (ditto)' +
      LineEnding +
      'Please notice the option is just a suggestion, some targets may ignore it and have just tape or just disc support.',
    'SAVEAT' +
      LineEnding +
      'Saves print position for current window. This allows you to maintain the print position for example while printing elsewhere in the window. You should consider using a separate window for most tasks. ' +
      'This may find use in the creation of a new input line or in animation sequences...',
    'SET flagno.' +
      LineEnding +
      'Flag flagno. is set to 255.',
    'SETAT attrno. operation' +
      LineEnding +
      'This condacts set,clears or toggle bits of flags in the range 28-59. Just like in HASAT/HASNAT, the bit is calculated by counting downwards from flag 59, 8 bits per flag, so if attrno is 0-7, the bits' +
      ' of flag 59 are affected, if it is 8-15, the bits of flag 58 are affected, and so on.' +
      LineEnding +
      'Operation can be:' +
      LineEnding +
      '0 - Clear the bit.' +
      LineEnding +
      '1 - Set the bit.' +
      LineEnding +
      '2 - Toggle the bit.' +
      LineEnding +
      'IMPORTANT:Please notice SETAT modifies the flags, but doesn''t change anything the flags represent regarding the object''s attributes.',
    'SETCO objno.' +
      LineEnding +
      'Sets the currently referenced object to objno.',
    'SFX value value' +
      LineEnding +
      'SFX is explained in depth in the multimedia manual.',
    'SKIP distance | label' +
      LineEnding +
      'where distance is -128 to 128, or to the specified label.' +
      LineEnding +
      'Will move the current entry in a table back or forward. Parameter -1 means restart current entry, -2 the previous one, etc. It also can jump forward where 0 is next entry, 1 the one after, etc. There ' +
      'is no error checking so it should be possible to jump out of the table (what would be fatal).' +
      LineEnding +
      'Skip can also accept as a parameter a local symbol. This is a symbol preceded by a $ sign. They are local to each process table and will not affect any global symbols used. The big advantage is that t' +
      'hey can forward reference.' +
      LineEnding +
      'This is implemented with rear patching. The symbol is defined by placing it in the source file immediately before the entry it refers to:' +
      LineEnding +
      'e.g.' +
      LineEnding +
      '$backloop' +
      LineEnding +
      '> _ _' +
      LineEnding +
      'PRINT Flag' +
      LineEnding +
      'MINUS Flag 1' +
      LineEnding +
      'NOTZERO Flag' +
      LineEnding +
      'SKIP $backloop' +
      LineEnding +
      '> _ _' +
      LineEnding +
      'ZERO Error' +
      LineEnding +
      'SKIP $Forward' +
      LineEnding +
      '> _ _' +
      LineEnding +
      'EXIT 0' +
      LineEnding +
      '$Forward' +
      LineEnding +
      '> _ _' +
      LineEnding +
      '?',
    'SMALLER flagno1. flagno2.' +
      LineEnding +
      'Succeeds if flag flagno1 is smaller than flag flagno2. It is not actually needed as reversing the parameters of BIGGER would do the same, but it may make programs more readable and indirection can be ' +
      'used with ''either'' flag by using the appropriate condition.',
    'SPACE' +
      LineEnding +
      'Will simply print a space to the current output stream. Shorter than MES Space!',
    'SUB flagno1. flagno2.' +
      LineEnding +
      'Flag flagno 2 has the contents of Flag flagno 1 subtracted from it. If the result is negative the flag is set to 0.',
    'SWAP objno1. objno2.' +
      LineEnding +
      'The positions of the two objects are exchanged. Flag 1 is not adjusted. The currently referenced object is set to be Object objno 2.',
    'SYNONYM verb noun' +
      LineEnding +
      'Substitutes the given verb and noun in the LS. Nullword (Usually ''_'') can be used to suppress substitution for one or the other - or both I suppose!' +
      LineEnding +
      'e.g. MATCH ON SYNONYM LIGHT MATCH' +
      LineEnding +
      '> STRIKE MATCH' +
      LineEnding +
      'SYNONYM LIGHT _' +
      LineEnding +
      '> LIGHT MATCH' +
      LineEnding +
      '; Actions...' +
      LineEnding +
      'will switch the player orderinto a standard format for several different entries. Allowing only one to deal with the actual actions.' +
      LineEnding +
      'Please notice unlike other actions, SYNONYM does not mark the internal done flag. That means that, for instance, a ISDONE condition would fail after SYNONYM unless another action was executed before.',
    'SYSMESS sysno.' +
      LineEnding +
      'SYSMESS "string"' +
      LineEnding +
      'Prints System Message sysno.' +
      LineEnding +
      'Prints message between quotes.',
    'TAB col' +
      LineEnding +
      'Sets current print position to given column on current line.',
    'TAKEOUT objno. locno+' +
      LineEnding +
      'If Object objno. is worn or carried, SM25 ("I already have the _.") is printed and actions NEWTEXT & DONE are performed.' +
      LineEnding +
      'If Object objno. is at the current location, SM45 ("The _ isn''t in the"), a description of Object locno. and SM51 (".") is printed and actions NEWTEXT & DONE are performed.' +
      LineEnding +
      'If Object objno. is not at the current location and not at Location locno. then SM52 ("There isn''t one of those in the"), a description of Object locno. and SM51 (".") is printed and actions NEWTEXT &' +
      ' DONE are performed.' +
      LineEnding +
      'If Object locno. is not carried or worn, and the total weight of the objects carried and worn by the player plus Object objno. would exceed the maximum conveyable weight (Flag 52) then SM43 ("The _ we' +
      'ighs too much for me.") is printed and actions NEWTEXT & DONE are performed.' +
      LineEnding +
      'If the maximum number of objects is being carried (Flag 1 is greater than, or the same as, Flag 37), SM27 ("I can''t carry any more things.") is printed and actions NEWTEXT & DONE are performed. In add' +
      'ition, any current DOALL loop is cancelled.' +
      LineEnding +
      'Otherwise the position of Object objno. is changed to carried, Flag 1 is incremented and SM36 ("I now have the _.") is printed.' +
      LineEnding +
      'Note: No check is made, by either PUTIN or TAKEOUT, that Object locno. is present. This must be carried out by yourself if required.',
    'TIME duration option' +
      LineEnding +
      'Allows input to be set to ''timeout'' after a specific duration in 1 second intervals, i.e. the Process 2 table will be called again if the player types nothing for the specified period. This action alt' +
      'ers flags 48 & 49. ''option'' allows this to also occur on ANYKEY and the "More..." prompt. In order to calculate the number to use for the option just add the numbers shown next to each item to achieve' +
      ' the required combination.' +
      LineEnding +
      '1 - While waiting for first character of Input only.' +
      LineEnding +
      '2 - While waiting for the key on the "More..." prompt.' +
      LineEnding +
      '4 - While waiting for the key on the ANYKEY action.' +
      LineEnding +
      'e.g. TIME 5 6 (option = 2+4) will allow 5 seconds of inactivity on behalf of the player on input, ANYKEY or "More..." and between each key press. Whereas TIME 5 3 (option = 1+2) allows it only on the ' +
      'first character of input and on "More...".' +
      LineEnding +
      'TIME 0 0 will stop timeouts (default).',
    'WEAR objno.' +
      LineEnding +
      'If Object objno. is at the current location (but not carried or worn) SM49 ("I don''t have the _.") is printed and actions NEWTEXT & DONE are performed.' +
      LineEnding +
      'If Object objno. is worn, SM29 ("I''m already wearing the _.") is printed and actions NEWTEXT & DONE are performed.' +
      LineEnding +
      'If Object objno. is not carried, SM28 ("I don''t have one of those.") is printed and actions NEWTEXT & DONE are performed.' +
      LineEnding +
      'If Object objno. is not wearable (as specified in the object definition section) then SM40 ("I can''t wear the _.") is printed and actions NEWTEXT & DONE are performed.' +
      LineEnding +
      'Otherwise the position of Object objno. is changed to worn, Flag 1 is decremented and SM37 ("I''m now wearing the _.") is printed.',
    'WEIGH objno. flagno.' +
      LineEnding +
      'The true weight of Object objno. is calculated (i.e. if it is a container, any objects inside have their weight added - do not forget that nested containers stop adding their contents after ten levels' +
      ') and the value is placed in Flag flagno. This will have a maximum value of 255 which will not be exceeded. If Object objno. is a container of zero weight, Flag flagno. will be cleared as objects in z' +
      'ero weight containers, also weigh zero!',
    'WEIGHT flagno.' +
      LineEnding +
      'Calculates the true weight of all objects carried and worn by the player (i.e. any containers will have the weight of their content added up to a maximum of 255), this value is then placed in Flag fla' +
      'gno. This would be useful to ensure the player was not carrying too much weight to cross a bridge without it collapsing etc.',
    'WHATO' +
      LineEnding +
      'A search for the object number represented by Noun (Adjective)1 is made in the object definition section in order of location priority; carried, worn, here. This is because it is assumed any use of WH' +
      'ATO will be related to carried objects rather than any that are worn or here. If an object is found its number is placed in flag 51, along with the standard current object parameters in flags 54-57. T' +
      'his allows you to create other auto actions (the tutorial gives an example of this for dropping objects in the tree).',
    'WINAT line col' +
      LineEnding +
      'Sets current window to start at given line and column. Clipping height and width to fit available screen.',
    'WINDOW window' +
      LineEnding +
      'Selects window (0-7) as current print output stream.',
    'WINSIZE height width' +
      LineEnding +
      'Sets current window size to given height and width. Clipping as needed to fit available screen.',
    'WORN objno.' +
      LineEnding +
      'Succeeds if object objno. is worn.',
    'XDATA' +
      LineEnding +
      'The XDATA meta-condact serves to shorten a series of LETs. The first value is a flag number, and the rest are values to assign to that flag and the following ones.' +
      LineEnding +
      'For example:' +
      LineEnding +
      'XDATA "100,2,8"' +
      LineEnding +
      'becomes:' +
      LineEnding +
      'LET 100 2' +
      LineEnding +
      'LET 101 8' +
      LineEnding +
      'It''s very useful when you want to organize certain data in flags and makes it easier to write lots of LETs. For example, in an RPG you might have CON, INT, and DEX for 8 types of enemies starting at f' +
      'lag 100. You could write:' +
      LineEnding +
      'XDATA "100, 2,8,9, 3,3,7, 6,10,4, 5,1,40, 6,2,3, 104,5,4, 8,5,12"' +
      LineEnding +
      'And this would be compiled to:' +
      LineEnding +
      'LET 100 2' +
      LineEnding +
      'LET 101 8' +
      LineEnding +
      'LET 102 9' +
      LineEnding +
      'LET 103 3' +
      LineEnding +
      'LET 104 3' +
      LineEnding +
      'LET 105 7' +
      LineEnding +
      'LET 106 6' +
      LineEnding +
      'LET 107 10' +
      LineEnding +
      'LET 108 4' +
      LineEnding +
      'LET 109 5' +
      LineEnding +
      'LET 110 1' +
      LineEnding +
      'LET 111 40' +
      LineEnding +
      'LET 112 6' +
      LineEnding +
      'LET 113 2' +
      LineEnding +
      'LET 114 3' +
      LineEnding +
      'LET 115 104' +
      LineEnding +
      'LET 116 5' +
      LineEnding +
      'LET 117 4' +
      LineEnding +
      'LET 118 8' +
      LineEnding +
      'LET 119 5' +
      LineEnding +
      'LET 120 12' +
      LineEnding +
      'As you can see, you separate the values by comma, but you can also leave empty space to separate data visually.',
    'XMES "string"' +
      LineEnding +
      'Writes the string in quotes. This is an alternate text mode that you can use if you run out of messages, but XMES has the following limitations:' +
      LineEnding +
      'Each message may not exceed 511 characters.' +
      LineEnding +
      'All messages used by XMES and XMESSAGE must not exceed 64K once compressed.' +
      LineEnding +
      'These xmessages are read from disk on demand, so depending on the reading device, response time may be slow. Use them with care. Only on Spectrum 128K are xmes stored in RAM.' +
      LineEnding +
      'This also means that if you''re using split-mode graphics (see below), visual glitches or screen blackening may occur while reading from disk. If you must use XMES in a long adventure, avoid split-mode' +
      ' if possible.',
    'XMESSSAGE "string"' +
      LineEnding +
      'Same as XMES but adds a NEWLINE at the end.',
    'XPLAY "string"' +
      LineEnding +
      'DAAD can make sounds with the BEEP condact, which is handy for a beep, but not ideal for melodies - even short ones.' +
      LineEnding +
      'XPLAY lets you specify a melody in a format closer to music notation, which the compiler converts into a series of BEEPs and PAUSEs (for silences), which DAAD understands.' +
      LineEnding +
      'Only one channel is supported.' +
      LineEnding +
      'The expected string is a subset of the MML format (see below).',
    'XSPLITSCR value' +
      LineEnding +
      'For machines that support split screen (currently Amstrad CPC and Commodore 64), this condact replaces GFX value 15.' +
      LineEnding +
      'Split screen means having a different graphics mode at the top of the screen (where images appear) and at the bottom (where text is shown).' +
      LineEnding +
      'This is what "value" means for each machine:' +
      LineEnding +
      'MachinevalueTop modeBottom mode CPC0CPC Mode 1CPC Mode 1 CPC1CPC Mode 0CPC Mode 1 CPC2CPC Mode 2CPC Mode 1 C640C64 HiResC64 HiRes C641C64 MulticolorC64 Hires The screen always split at scanline 96, th' +
      'at is unavoidable.',
    'ZERO flagno.' +
      LineEnding +
      'Succeeds if flag flagno. is set to zero.');
